<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nathaniel Hawthorne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nathaniel Hathorne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nathaniel Hawthorne was an American novelist and short story writer, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-massachusetts">Salem, Massachusetts</a>, whose works often focus on history, morality and religion. After graduating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bowdoin-college">Bowdoin College</a> in 1825 he published the novel Fanshawe in 1828, which he later tried to suppress, and collected his stories as Twice-Told Tales in 1837. He worked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> Custom House and joined the transcendentalist community of Brook Farm before marrying Sophia Peabody in 1842. The Scarlet Letter appeared in 1850. Much of his fiction centres on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> and belongs to dark romanticism; he also wrote a campaign biography of his college friend Franklin Pierce.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nathaniel-hawthorne.jpg" alt="Nathaniel Hawthorne" width="330" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Benjamin Brady · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nathaniel_Hawthorne_by_Brady,_1860-64.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hawthorne was born Nathaniel Hathorne on 4 July 1804 in Salem. His ancestor William Hathorne, the first of the family to emigrate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, became a magistrate of the Massachusetts Bay Colony, infamous for harsh sentencing, and William's son John was one of the judges at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salem-witch-trials">Salem witch trials</a>. Hawthorne probably added the w to his surname in his early twenties to distance himself from them. His father, a sea captain, died of yellow fever in Dutch Suriname in 1808, and his widow took the family to live with relatives in Salem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Fanshawe appeared anonymously in October 1828, printed at the author's own expense of $100. Reviews were generally positive but it sold poorly. On 17 January 1839 he accepted a post at the Boston Custom House. Writing in what he called his owl's nest in the family home, he later said he had not lived but only dreamed about living. Horatio Bridge offered to cover the risk of collecting his stories into Twice-Told Tales, which made him known locally.</p><p>He joined Brook Farm in 1841, to save money to marry Sophia Peabody, whom he married on 9 July 1842. They lived three years at the Old Manse in Concord, where he wrote most of the tales of Mosses from an Old Manse. In April 1846 he became surveyor of the port of Salem, and he lost the post after the change of administration in 1848. The Scarlet Letter followed in 1850, and James T. Fields had persuaded him to make it a novel rather than a short story.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His four major romances were The Scarlet Letter (1850), The House of the Seven Gables (1851), The Blithedale Romance (1852) and The Marble Faun (1860). His fiction belongs to dark romanticism, treating guilt, sin and evil as inherent in humanity, and draws on Puritan New England for themes of ancestral sin and retribution.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herman-melville">Herman Melville</a> praised him in the review Hawthorne and His Mosses, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-allan-poe">Edgar Allan Poe</a> wrote that his style was purity itself. He died in his sleep on 19 May 1864 at Plymouth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>, during a trip with Franklin Pierce, whose campaign biography he had written, and was buried on Authors' Ridge in Sleepy Hollow Cemetery, Concord.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nathaniel Hawthorne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
